{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R769b6zUxyf3x7WWyCf5eA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\timport { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\n2\tconst name = process.argv.includes('--development') ? 'development' : 'production';\n3\tconst configuration = JSON.parse(await readFile(new URL('../config/' + name + '.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));\n4\tconst summary = {\n5\t  profile: name,\n6\t  listen: configuration.listen,\n7\t  port: configuration.port,\n8\t  transport: configuration.transportModule,\n9\t  allowedHosts: configuration.allowedHosts,\n10\t  allowedTools: configuration.allowedTools,\n11\t  dataRoot: configuration.dataRoot\n12\t};\n13\tconsole.log(JSON.stringify(summary, null, 2));\n14\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a50c549d-5819-44eb-ad3e-436276569e1b","uuid":"e3eaa800-87e6-4803-a035-a42724698cbe","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:47:54.414Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/tools/inventory.mjs","content":"import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nconst name = process.argv.includes('--development') ? 'development' : 'production';\nconst configuration = JSON.parse(await readFile(new URL('../config/' + name + '.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));\nconst summary = {\n  profile: name,\n  listen: configuration.listen,\n  port: configuration.port,\n  transport: configuration.transportModule,\n  allowedHosts: configuration.allowedHosts,\n  allowedTools: configuration.allowedTools,\n  dataRoot: configuration.dataRoot\n};\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(summary, null, 2));\n","numLines":14,"startLine":1,"totalLines":14}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01R769b6zUxyf3x7WWyCf5eA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012e78EfMtqiLjwkaytYdJsG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Relay operations\n2\t\n3\t## Deployment review\n4\t\n5\tProvision a dedicated service account for the relay process.\n6\tProvision a document root owned by that account.\n7\tKeep the root outside the web server's executable directories.\n8\tReplace the synthetic gateway token with deployment configuration.\n9\tBind the relay to the configured loopback address.\n10\tDeploy a gateway that strips browser-supplied relay headers.\n11\tThe gateway must emit project memberships from an authoritative identity source.\n12\tMembership expiry is owned by the gateway and directory integration.\n13\tNo directory client or token renewal implementation exists in this repository.\n14\tThe relay does not call an identity service during individual operations.\n15\tAn identity outage policy therefore belongs to the gateway deployment.\n16\t\n17\t## Client operations\n18\t\n19\tContributors use the gateway to publish documents and collect source material.\n20\tThe gateway supplies the user's current project memberships.\n21\tClients select a project using the documented lowercase identifier.\n22\tClients receive JSON responses for all supplied operations.\n23\tPublication and source collection return an event object on completion.\n24\tThe tool route returns a small operation-specific status object.\n25\tThere is no asynchronous polling or background-job identifier.\n26\tA successful response applies to the one submitted operation.\n27\tA failed operation does not yield a successful event response.\n28\tA caller should not treat a response timeout as proof that no write occurred.\n29\tNo idempotency key is part of the supplied protocol.\n30\t\n31\t## Document recovery\n32\t\n33\tThe document adapter writes one UTF-8 file per document identifier.\n34\tThe file extension is always `.txt`.\n35\tThe document identifier cannot contain a slash or dot.\n36\tThe project identifier cannot contain a slash or dot.\n37\tThe adapter recursively creates the selected project directory.\n38\tDocument text is stored literally and is not evaluated by the relay.\n39\tThere is no template engine, Markdown renderer, or browser preview in this service.\n40\tFuture downstream renderers need their own content handling rules.\n41\tBackups and retention are deployment responsibilities.\n42\tThe supplied code does not implement document history.\n43\t\n44\t## Source collection diagnostics\n45\t\n46\tThe recording adapter is useful for inspecting selected URLs without network traffic.\n47\tIt records the exact value passed to `retrieve`.\n48\tIt does not emulate DNS, redirects, or TLS validation.\n49\tA recording-adapter test cannot prove production network isolation.\n50\tThe production adapter selects HTTP or HTTPS from the URL protocol.\n51\tIt rejects other protocols before making a request.\n52\tIt uses the URL's host and port for the outbound request.\n53\tThe host resolver is provided by the operating system.\n54\tOperational DNS changes take effect according to the host resolver configuration.\n55\tRequests carry no gateway token or identity header to the upstream.\n56\tThe upstream body is returned as text rather than interpreted as JavaScript.\n57\tThe supplied size and timeout limits apply to retrieval resource use.\n58\tUpstream connection failures are returned as operation errors.\n59\t\n60\t## Tool inventory\n61\t\n62\tThe status operation returns a static ready state for the project.\n63\tThe refresh operation returns a synchronous refresh acknowledgement.\n64\tNeither launches a command or deletes project data.\n65\tThe maintenance erase operation removes a project directory recursively.\n66\tThe production dispatcher denies that operation by name before lookup.\n67\tAdding erase to an allowlist would change the public capability boundary.\n68\tSuch a deployment must have an explicit authorization review.\n69\tThe request protocol cannot add entries to the configured allowlist.\n70\t\n71\t## External boundaries\n72\t\n73\tThe gateway code is not part of the review input.\n74\tThe content provider code is not part of the review input.\n75\tThe host resolver and egress policy are not represented as enforcement modules.\n76\tThe audit collector is not implemented here.\n77\tDeployment owners should retain a record of gateway and provider configuration.\n78\tAccess reviews should cover current project memberships.\n79\tNetwork inventories should include the content provider and internal services.\n80\tThe service package does not contain the deployment network inventory.\n81\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a50c549d-5819-44eb-ad3e-436276569e1b","uuid":"2f0b598f-f1db-463b-b104-1b128f49d450","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:47:54.415Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/docs/operations.md","content":"# Relay operations\n\n## Deployment review\n\nProvision a dedicated service account for the relay process.\nProvision a document root owned by that account.\nKeep the root outside the web server's executable directories.\nReplace the synthetic gateway token with deployment configuration.\nBind the relay to the configured loopback address.\nDeploy a gateway that strips browser-supplied relay headers.\nThe gateway must emit project memberships from an authoritative identity source.\nMembership expiry is owned by the gateway and directory integration.\nNo directory client or token renewal implementation exists in this repository.\nThe relay does not call an identity service during individual operations.\nAn identity outage policy therefore belongs to the gateway deployment.\n\n## Client operations\n\nContributors use the gateway to publish documents and collect source material.\nThe gateway supplies the user's current project memberships.\nClients select a project using the documented lowercase identifier.\nClients receive JSON responses for all supplied operations.\nPublication and source collection return an event object on completion.\nThe tool route returns a small operation-specific status object.\nThere is no asynchronous polling or background-job identifier.\nA successful response applies to the one submitted operation.\nA failed operation does not yield a successful event response.\nA caller should not treat a response timeout as proof that no write occurred.\nNo idempotency key is part of the supplied protocol.\n\n## Document recovery\n\nThe document adapter writes one UTF-8 file per document identifier.\nThe file extension is always `.txt`.\nThe document identifier cannot contain a slash or dot.\nThe project identifier cannot contain a slash or dot.\nThe adapter recursively creates the selected project directory.\nDocument text is stored literally and is not evaluated by the relay.\nThere is no template engine, Markdown renderer, or browser preview in this service.\nFuture downstream renderers need their own content handling rules.\nBackups and retention are deployment responsibilities.\nThe supplied code does not implement document history.\n\n## Source collection diagnostics\n\nThe recording adapter is useful for inspecting selected URLs without network traffic.\nIt records the exact value passed to `retrieve`.\nIt does not emulate DNS, redirects, or TLS validation.\nA recording-adapter test cannot prove production network isolation.\nThe production adapter selects HTTP or HTTPS from the URL protocol.\nIt rejects other protocols before making a request.\nIt uses the URL's host and port for the outbound request.\nThe host resolver is provided by the operating system.\nOperational DNS changes take effect according to the host resolver configuration.\nRequests carry no gateway token or identity header to the upstream.\nThe upstream body is returned as text rather than interpreted as JavaScript.\nThe supplied size and timeout limits apply to retrieval resource use.\nUpstream connection failures are returned as operation errors.\n\n## Tool inventory\n\nThe status operation returns a static ready state for the project.\nThe refresh operation returns a synchronous refresh acknowledgement.\nNeither launches a command or deletes project data.\nThe maintenance erase operation removes a project directory recursively.\nThe production dispatcher denies that operation by name before lookup.\nAdding erase to an allowlist would change the public capability boundary.\nSuch a deployment must have an explicit authorization review.\nThe request protocol cannot add entries to the configured allowlist.\n\n## External boundaries\n\nThe gateway code is not part of the review input.\nThe content provider code is not part of the review input.\nThe host resolver and egress policy are not represented as enforcement modules.\nThe audit collector is not implemented here.\nDeployment owners should retain a record of gateway and provider configuration.\nAccess reviews should cover current project memberships.\nNetwork inventories should include the content provider and internal services.\nThe service package does not contain the deployment network inventory.\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012e78EfMtqiLjwkaytYdJsG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
